---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';

const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['choose', 'Choose a method'],
  ['lan', 'Same network (LAN)'],
  ['tailscale', 'Tailscale Serve'],
  ['cloudflare', 'Cloudflare Tunnel'],
  ['funnel', 'Tailscale Funnel'],
  ['both', 'At the office and away'],
  ['security', 'Security'],
  ['problems', 'Problems'],
];

// One route from a device to mobd, drawn as a row of boxes (no inline styles: the CSP forbids them).
const routes = {
  lan: ['Phone or laptop', 'Office Wi-Fi', 'Office computer · mobd'],
  tailscale: ['Phone with Tailscale', 'Your private tailnet (encrypted, usually direct)', 'Office computer · Tailscale Serve → mobd'],
  cloudflare: ['Phone, no extra app', 'Cloudflare (HTTPS, your domain)', 'Office computer · cloudflared → mobd'],
  funnel: ['Phone, no extra app', 'Tailscale relays (public HTTPS)', 'Office computer · Tailscale Funnel → mobd'],
};
---
<DocLayout title="Remote access" description="Reach mobd on your office computer (macOS or Linux) from anywhere: on the office network, through Tailscale, or through a Cloudflare Tunnel." lead="How your phone and laptop reach mobd on the office computer: on the office network, or from anywhere. Set the route up once, then pair each device with <code>mobd link</code>." sections={sections}>
      <section id="choose">
        <h2>Choose a method</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Same network</th><th scope="col">Tailscale Serve</th><th scope="col">Cloudflare Tunnel</th><th scope="col">Tailscale Funnel</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Works from</th><td>office network only</td><td>anywhere</td><td>anywhere</td><td>anywhere</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">App on the phone</th><td>none</td><td>Tailscale</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">You need</th><td>nothing</td><td>a Tailscale account</td><td>a Cloudflare account and a domain</td><td>a Tailscale account</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Who can reach mobd</th><td>your network</td><td>only your devices</td><td>anyone, pairing required</td><td>anyone, pairing required</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Who can read traffic</th><td>your network (plain HTTP)</td><td>only you</td><td>you and Cloudflare</td><td>only you</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Live view</th><td>WebRTC</td><td>WebRTC</td><td>WebSocket</td><td>WebSocket</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Speed</th><td>fastest</td><td>fast when direct</td><td>good</td><td>slower (relayed)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Start with the same network.</strong> It needs no setup and is the fastest.</li>
          <li><strong>To use Mob from anywhere,</strong> choose <strong>Tailscale Serve</strong> if you're happy to run the Tailscale app on your phone: only your devices can reach mobd. Otherwise choose <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Funnel</strong> is for occasional use without a domain; it's slower than the others.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Whatever you choose, every device must pair: an address alone gives nobody access to mobd.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="lan">
        <h2>Same network (LAN)</h2>
        <div class="route">{routes.lan.map((r, i) => <><span>{r}</span>{i < routes.lan.length - 1 && <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>}</>)}</div>
        <p>For devices on the office Wi-Fi. On the office computer:</p>
        <pre>mobd link</pre>
        <p>Without remote access set up, it prints the office computer's network address with a QR code: <code>http://&lt;name&gt;.local:8080</code> on a Mac, the computer's network IP on Linux, such as <code>http://192.168.1.20:8080</code>. Scan it in Mob (<strong>Settings → Machines → Add machine</strong>). Once remote access is set up, <code>mobd link</code> uses that address instead; <code>mobd link --lan</code> always gives the network one.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>mobd must listen on the network.</strong> <code>mobd init</code> sets <code>"listen": "0.0.0.0:8080"</code>. If yours is <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>, <code>mobd link</code> says so instead of printing a code that cannot work.</li>
          <li><strong>It is plain HTTP.</strong> Use it on a network you trust, never forward port 8080 to the internet.</li>
          <li><strong>Firewall:</strong> on macOS, if the firewall asks, allow mobd to accept incoming connections. On Linux, open port 8080 and UDP 9527 to your network (<a href="/install/linux/#firewall">how</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="tailscale">
        <h2>Tailscale Serve</h2>
        <div class="route">{routes.tailscale.map((r, i) => <><span>{r}</span>{i < routes.tailscale.length - 1 && <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>}</>)}</div>
        <p>Your devices join a private network (a tailnet) with the office computer. mobd gets an HTTPS address that only they can reach, and the live view keeps WebRTC.</p>
        <h3>What you need</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://tailscale.com/download" rel="noreferrer">Tailscale</a> on the office computer and on each device, signed in to the <strong>same account</strong>. On Linux: <code>curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh</code>, then <code>sudo tailscale up</code>.</li>
          <li>In the <a href="https://login.tailscale.com/admin/dns" rel="noreferrer">admin console → DNS</a>: <strong>MagicDNS</strong> and <strong>HTTPS Certificates</strong> turned on.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Set it up</h3>
        <pre>mobd remote setup --tailscale</pre>
        <p>It checks Tailscale is installed and signed in and that HTTPS is on, shows the <code>tailscale serve</code> command and asks before running it, then checks the new address reaches this mobd and saves it. Then pair each device with Tailscale switched on:</p>
        <pre>mobd link</pre>
        <details class="fold"><summary>By hand</summary>
          <pre>sudo tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:8080
tailscale serve status</pre>
          <p>The status shows the address, such as <code>https://office.your-tailnet.ts.net</code>. Pair with it, without <code>:8080</code>: <code>mobd link --url https://office.your-tailnet.ts.net</code>. With the Tailscale app on macOS, <code>sudo</code> is not needed. On Linux, <code>sudo tailscale set --operator=$USER</code> once lets your user run it without <code>sudo</code>.</p>
        </details>
        <h3>Check it is direct</h3>
        <pre>tailscale ping your-phone</pre>
        <p><strong>via direct</strong> or <strong>via</strong> an address and port means the device talks to the office computer directly. <strong>via DERP(…)</strong> means it goes through a Tailscale relay: it works, but requests can take seconds when the relay is far away. Give it a minute, or try another network.</p>
        <details class="fold"><summary>Only your devices (access control)</summary>
          <p>In <a href="https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls" rel="noreferrer">access controls</a>, allow only your devices to reach TCP 443 and UDP 9527 on the office computer: 443 carries conversations and pairing, 9527 the live view over WebRTC. With 443 only, the live view uses WebSocket.</p>
        </details>
        <h3>Turn it off</h3>
        <p><code>mobd remote off</code>, or by hand <code>sudo tailscale serve --https=443 off</code>. Paired devices stay paired.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="cloudflare">
        <h2>Cloudflare Tunnel</h2>
        <div class="route">{routes.cloudflare.map((r, i) => <><span>{r}</span>{i < routes.cloudflare.length - 1 && <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>}</>)}</div>
        <p>Your devices reach mobd at an HTTPS address on your own domain, with no app besides Mob. The office computer runs <code>cloudflared</code>, which connects out to Cloudflare, so it needs no public IP or open port. The tunnel carries no UDP, so the live view uses the WebSocket stream.</p>
        <h3>What you need</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>A Cloudflare account and a domain whose DNS is on Cloudflare; below, <code>mob.example.com</code>.</li>
          <li>In the <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/" rel="noreferrer">Cloudflare dashboard</a>, <strong>Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels</strong>: create a <strong>Cloudflared</strong> tunnel, environment <strong>macOS</strong> or <strong>Debian</strong>, and copy the token from the install command it shows.</li>
          <li>On the tunnel, add a <strong>public hostname</strong>: <code>mob.example.com</code>, service type <strong>HTTP</strong>, URL <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>. HTTP, not HTTPS: Cloudflare provides the certificate.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Set it up</h3>
        <pre>mobd remote setup --cloudflare --hostname mob.example.com</pre>
        <p>It asks for the token without showing it, installs <code>cloudflared</code> if needed (Homebrew on macOS, Cloudflare's apt repository on Debian and Ubuntu), installs it as a service that starts at boot (asking before each command), waits for the tunnel, checks <code>https://mob.example.com</code> reaches this mobd and saves it. Then pair each device:</p>
        <pre>mobd link</pre>
        <details class="fold"><summary>By hand</summary>
          <p>Install <code>cloudflared</code>: on macOS <code>brew install cloudflared</code>; on Debian and Ubuntu from <a href="https://pkg.cloudflare.com/" rel="noreferrer">Cloudflare's apt repository</a>; elsewhere from Cloudflare's <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/connections/connect-networks/downloads/" rel="noreferrer">downloads</a>. Then:</p>
          <pre>sudo cloudflared service install YOUR_TUNNEL_TOKEN
mobd link --url https://mob.example.com</pre>
          <p>The tunnel shows <strong>Healthy</strong> in the dashboard once <code>cloudflared</code> is connected.</p>
        </details>
        <p>If devices only use the tunnel, set <code>listen</code> to <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code> and run <code>mobd restart</code>, so the unencrypted port isn't open on your network. Keep the tunnel token secret; regenerate it in the dashboard if it leaks.</p>
        <h3>Turn it off</h3>
        <p><code>mobd remote off</code> removes the service from the office computer (by hand: <code>sudo cloudflared service uninstall</code>). The tunnel and hostname stay in your Cloudflare account until you delete them there. Paired devices stay paired.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="funnel">
        <h2>Tailscale Funnel</h2>
        <div class="route">{routes.funnel.map((r, i) => <><span>{r}</span>{i < routes.funnel.length - 1 && <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>}</>)}</div>
        <p>A public HTTPS address from Tailscale for devices without the Tailscale app, with no domain needed. Traffic always goes through Tailscale's relays, so requests can take seconds when they are far away. <code>mobd remote</code> doesn't set it up; by hand:</p>
        <pre>sudo tailscale funnel --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:8080
sudo tailscale funnel status
mobd link --url https://office.your-tailnet.ts.net</pre>
        <p>Serve and Funnel cannot share a port. To move to Serve once your devices have Tailscale, run <code>sudo tailscale funnel --https=443 off</code>, then the Serve setup; the address stays the same, so devices stay paired. See <a href="https://tailscale.com/docs/features/tailscale-funnel" rel="noreferrer">Tailscale Funnel</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="both">
        <h2>At the office and away</h2>
        <p>An office computer can have more than one address: the fast network address at the office and a remote one for elsewhere. Scan a code for each:</p>
        <pre>mobd link --lan   # scan: the office network address
mobd link         # scan: the remote address</pre>
        <p>The second code for the same computer adds its address to its row in <strong>Settings → Machines</strong>, labelled <strong>LAN</strong> or <strong>Remote</strong>; there is still one row, with the same conversations. The row shows which route is in use and whether it answers. To switch, long-press the computer in the list (on the desktop app, click the route next to its name), or tap the route under the chat title. When the route in use doesn't answer, Mob shows <strong>LAN not reachable · Use Remote</strong>; it never switches by itself. Each address is paired on its own, so the device credential only goes to addresses it was paired on.</p>
        <p>With Tailscale or Cloudflare the remote address also works at the office, so one address is often enough. The LAN address mainly makes the live view faster at your desk.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="security">
        <h2>Security</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Every request needs a paired device.</strong> Without its credential, mobd answers nothing but the pairing request, whatever the route.</li>
          <li><strong>Pairing is closed unless you open it.</strong> <code>mobd link</code> creates one invitation: 256-bit, one use, valid for 10 minutes, and only on the office computer. With no open invitation, mobd refuses every pairing attempt and logs it. Run <code>mobd link --close</code> once your device is added.</li>
          <li><strong>A public address</strong> (Cloudflare Tunnel, Funnel) is reachable by anyone but useless without pairing. Keep mobd up to date. For Cloudflare, you can add a rate-limiting rule for <code>/pair</code> under <strong>Security → WAF</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Who can read the traffic:</strong> on the office network it is plain HTTP. Tailscale encrypts end to end. Cloudflare ends TLS on its servers, so invitations, credentials, conversations and the live view pass through it decrypted; if that isn't acceptable, use Tailscale Serve.</li>
          <li><strong>A lost device:</strong> <code>mobd clients list</code>, then <code>mobd clients revoke CLIENT_ID</code>. It is cut off on every route at once.</li>
          <li>Don't put <strong>Cloudflare Access</strong> in front of mobd for now: Mob can't sign in to it yet.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="problems">
        <h2>Problems</h2>
        <p>Start with this on the office computer. It shows the remote address and checks it reaches this mobd:</p>
        <pre>mobd remote</pre>
        <details class="fold"><summary>Mob says “Cannot reach mobd” or “mobd did not answer in time”</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>On the office computer, <code>mobd status</code> and <code>mobd logs</code>: is mobd running?</li>
            <li><strong>LAN address:</strong> is the device on the office network? Guest Wi-Fi often keeps devices apart. On Linux, check the firewall.</li>
            <li><strong>Tailscale:</strong> is Tailscale on and connected on the device? iPhone and iPad run one VPN at a time. <code>tailscale ping</code> should show a direct path.</li>
            <li><strong>Away from the office on the LAN address:</strong> Mob shows <strong>LAN not reachable · Use Remote</strong>; switch to the remote address.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>
        <details class="fold"><summary>HTTP 502</summary>
          <p>The route works but mobd doesn't answer behind it: mobd isn't running, or Serve or the tunnel's hostname points at a port other than mobd's (<code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code>).</p>
        </details>
        <details class="fold"><summary>Cloudflare error 1033</summary>
          <p><code>cloudflared</code> isn't running or can't reach Cloudflare. Check the tunnel's status in the dashboard, and the service on the office computer: <code>sudo launchctl list | grep cloudflared</code> on macOS, <code>systemctl status cloudflared</code> on Linux.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="fold"><summary>“Answers, but not as this mobd”</summary>
          <p><code>mobd remote</code> reached the address, but another program answered: often something else on port 8080 of the office computer. <code>lsof -nP -iTCP:8080 -sTCP:LISTEN</code> (macOS) or <code>sudo ss -ltnp 'sport = :8080'</code> (Linux) lists what listens there; move that program to another port.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="fold"><summary>Chat works, but the live view is slow</summary>
          <p>Over Cloudflare Tunnel and Funnel the live view always uses the WebSocket stream. Over Tailscale it uses WebRTC on UDP 9527; if your access control or a firewall blocks UDP, it falls back to WebSocket too.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="fold"><summary>The pairing link is “invalid, expired or already used”</summary>
          <p>Invitations last 10 minutes and work once. Run <code>mobd link</code> again and scan the new code.</p>
        </details>
        <p>Still stuck? See <a href="/faq/">FAQ &amp; help</a>, or <a href="/faq/#report">report a problem</a>.</p>
      </section>
</DocLayout>
